@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: what one run leaves behind to be read afterwards, and why the verdict has to be written before Do returns
' covers: internal/runlog/runlog.go
' covers: internal/ui/ui.go
' covers: internal/run/run.go (Do, recordOutcome)
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/sandbox.go (KeptLines, the failure path in Run)
' covers: internal/runlog/runlog_test.go
' covers: internal/run/outcome_test.go
' covers: internal/ui/ui_test.go
'   the three records the failure line offers . _spec/internal/sbx/sandbox-backend.puml
'   the tail's pty seam ...................... _spec/internal/ptyproxy/terminal-report-filter.puml
'   the work the same teardown rescues ....... _spec/internal/sbx/clone-workspace.puml
' Notes are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── THE TRANSCRIPT PROMISED SOMETHING IT DID NOT HOLD ──────────────────────
' `kept for diagnosis` points the operator at this file and promises "every line
' this run printed". It recorded neither the exit code nor the agent's output —
' the two things anyone opening it after a failure is there for.
'
' Both gaps have the same shape: the interesting event happened where the log
' could not see it.
'
'   THE EXIT CODE. `main` exits with the AGENT's own code via os.Exit and prints
'   nothing — deliberately, so a script wrapping proveo reads the agent's status
'   and not proveo's opinion of it. Any OTHER error is printed by `main` AFTER
'   `Do` has returned, and `Do` closes the log on its way out. So in the one
'   case there was nothing to write, and in the other there was nobody left to
'   write it.
'
'   THE AGENT'S LAST OUTPUT. The tail block was written straight to os.Stderr,
'   bypassing ui.TeeTo entirely. It reached the terminal, which is where an
'   operator sitting at the run already had it, and never reached the file the
'   same paragraph tells them to go and read.
'
' ── recordOutcome: WRITE THE VERDICT WHILE THE LOG IS STILL OPEN ───────────
' `Do` wraps its own result: `return recordOutcome(execute(...))`, inside `Do`,
' before the deferred Close. The channel it uses depends on who else is going to
' print, and getting that wrong doubles a line on screen instead of dropping it:
'
'   ExitError      through ui — terminal AND log. `main` prints nothing for
'                  this case, so ui is the only voice there is.
'   any other err  through ui.Logf — LOG ONLY. `main` still owns printing it,
'                  and a second copy on stderr would be proveo saying the same
'                  sentence twice.
'   nil            through ui.Logf. "the agent exited 0" is worth having in a
'                  transcript being diffed against a broken run, and worth
'                  nothing on a terminal that just watched it happen.
'   backend.Stopped  (since 2026-10-17) the default branch, ui.Logf: "outcome:
'                  stopped by SIGTERM" (or SIGHUP), LOG ONLY. `main` maps it to
'                  128+signal via exitCode and prints nothing, like ExitError;
'                  the terminal already showed Run's "stopped by SIGTERM —
'                  ending as on a normal exit" (MEASURED, run.go recordOutcome,
'                  outcome_test.go "a forwarded stop",
'                  _spec/internal/backend/exit-code.puml).
'
' The agent tail now goes through ui as well. Tail.push already strips ANSI and
' control characters, so what lands in the log is greppable — the same property
' the status tee exists to preserve, applied to the one block that had been
' routed around it.
'
' ── ONE ESCAPE GRAMMAR FOR THE LOG COPY (N5, DONE (wt) 2026-10-17) ─────────
' MEASURED: ui's run-log tee writes termtext.StripANSI(p) (internal/ui/ui.go:262),
' and Tail.push strips through the same function (internal/agentio/agentio.go:61)
' — one grammar, _spec/internal/agentio/agent-terminal-io.puml. The deleted
' ui.stripANSI skipped from ESC to the next 'm', so a NON-SGR sequence ate the
' text after it up to the next 'm': "\x1b[2Khello mom" was logged as "om"
' (measured by the coordinator before the fix). Test: TestTheRunLogKeepsTextAfterANonSGRSequence (ui_test.go:478).
'
' ── WHERE THE RUN'S ARTIFACTS LIVE (N13, DONE (wt) 2026-10-17) ─────────────
' The per-run state dir is <StateDir>/runs/<sid> (run.RunDirOf); the egDir
' parameters are runDir — runlog.Artifacts(runDir) (runlog.go:105) and
' sandbox.CapturePolicyLog(runDir, name) (sandbox.go:1800). Runs before N13
' wrote <StateDir>/egress/<sid>; `proveo clean` sweeps both for one release.
title Run transcript — making a denial diagnosable after the fact

node "PROVEO_HOME/logs" as HOME <<host>> {
  artifact "<sid>.log\nposture + teed status lines" as LOG <<db>>
  artifact "latest.log\nsymlink to the newest run" as LATEST <<db>>
}

node "proveo run" as RUN <<host>> {
  component "runlog.Open(sid)\nfirst statement in run.Do" as OPEN <<app>>
  component "ui.TeeTo\nstatus lines, termtext.StripANSI" as TEE <<app>>
  component "rl.Fields\nresolved posture" as FIELDS <<app>>
  component "sandbox.CapturePolicyLog\nteardown, before sbx rm\n(was captureSidecarLogs, docker)" as CAP <<app>>
  component "recordOutcome\ninside Do, while the log is open" as OUT <<app>>
  component "tail of the agent's last output\nANSI-stripped by Tail.push" as ATAIL <<app>>
}

component "main's os.Exit(agent code)\nprints nothing, and the log is closed" as OSEXIT <<error>>

database "run state dir <state>/runs/<sid>\nsbx/policy-log.json · sbx/kit\n(flows.ndjson · squid logs: docker, deleted)" as EG <<db>>

OPEN ARROW_MAIN LOG : create 0600
OPEN ARROW_OPTIONAL LATEST : relink
TEE ARROW_MAIN LOG
FIELDS ARROW_MAIN LOG
CAP ARROW_MAIN EG : sbx policy log → file
LOG ARROW_OPTIONAL EG : records where the evidence lives
OUT ARROW_MAIN TEE : ""ExitError"" → terminal + log
OUT ARROW_MAIN LOG : any other error → ""ui.Logf"", log only
ATAIL ARROW_MAIN TEE : through ui, no longer straight to stderr
OSEXIT ARROW_ERROR LOG : what recordOutcome runs ahead of

note right of OPEN
  **Opened before anything can fail.** A run that dies during
  bring-up is exactly the one worth a transcript, so the file
  exists before the manifest is read. A logging failure warns
  once and is then ignored: losing the transcript must never
  be the reason a run stops.
end note

note bottom of CAP
  **Why teardown is the only chance.** ""sbx policy log"" for a sandbox
  is gone once the sandbox is removed, so it is saved to
  ""<runDir>/sbx/policy-log.json"" first. That record — allowed and blocked
  hosts — is what lets an operator tell an sbx denial from a provider's
  own 401. HISTORY: on the deleted docker backend the inspector and Squid
  ran detached with ""--rm"" and ""docker logs"" was the sole copy.
end note

note as Blind #F4F9FB
  **The blind spot the transcript names explicitly.**

  sbx's policy log records hosts and decisions, never requests: there is
  no MITM of proveo's, so no flow record, no DLP and no body scan
  (posture ""observability"" says so in words). HISTORY: on docker,
  ""open"" + ""forward"" was the plain-bridge plan with nothing observing
  it; the other tiers wrote ""flows.ndjson"". Both are deleted.
end note

note right of FIELDS
  **Names and hosts only, never values.** The transcript records which
  keys were detected and which credential route ran (the "reachable
  hosts" row went with the tiers, 2026-10-16) — never a secret. Keys are meant to stay in sbx's secret
  store, and a troubleshooting file is not an exception.

  Fields are sorted so two runs of the same command diff to only what
  actually differed.
end note
note bottom of TEE
  **The tee changes what is recorded, never what the operator sees.** Every
  status line goes to both the terminal and the transcript, with the log
  copy stripped of escape sequences — a log full of ANSI is a log nobody
  greps.

  **The status vocabulary is one package for one reason:** proveo is a
  launcher that hands the terminal to the agent's own TUI, and the tmux
  agent-E2E layer screen-scrapes what proveo printed before that handover.
  So it stays strictly line-oriented, never a full-screen TUI of its own,
  and the ✓/⚠/❌ prefixes degrade to stable ""ok:"" / ""warn:"" / ""error:"" tags
  when the writer is not a terminal — which is what keeps CI logs and
  screen-scraping tests deterministic.

  Status lines go to **stderr** so stdout stays reserved for machine output:
  plans, lists, generated config.
end note

note as Tail #F4F9FB
  **The interactive run's missing channel, and why it was missing.**

  A tail of the agent's last output is normally taken by teeing os/exec's
  ""Stdout"" — and os/exec hands the child a real terminal only when that field
  holds an ""*os.File"". An ""io.MultiWriter"" substitutes a pipe, the agent can no
  longer read the window size, and its TUI draws one character per line. So an
  interactive run took NO tail, and "hands the terminal over unwrapped" and
  "takes no tail" were treated as one requirement.

  //They are not one requirement.// The interactive path already runs through
  ""internal/ptyproxy"", which gives the child its own pty and pumps the master
  back to the terminal — so the bytes are already passing through proveo.
  ""Proxy.OutTap"" copies them into the tail on the way past, at no cost to the
  child: the writers stay unwrapped and ""test -t 1"" still succeeds inside.

  Before this, a run that died at its prompt had no tail AND no transcript, and
  ""sandbox was stopped"" was the entire report.
end note

note bottom of LOG
  **A failed run names this file back.** The path travels into ""sandbox.Run"" on
  ""sandbox.Input.runLog"" and is printed beside the ""kept for diagnosis"" line,
  because the lines worth reading are the ones already gone: the resolved posture
  and the credential warnings print in the first seconds, and an agent can die a
  minute later. An operator who did not redirect stdout then has no route back to
  them. Naming the transcript at startup ONLY is naming it to someone who has no
  reason yet to look — see ""_spec/internal/sbx/sandbox-backend\.puml"" for the
  three records that failure line now offers.
end note

note bottom of OUT
  **The verdict, written before Do returns.** An
  ""ExitError"" goes through ui because ""main"" prints
  nothing for it; every other error goes to the LOG
  ONLY, because ""main"" still owns printing it and a
  second copy would say the same sentence twice.
end note

note bottom of OSEXIT
  **Why the log held no verdict.** ""main"" exits with
  the AGENT's code so a wrapping script reads that
  and not proveo's opinion — and any other error is
  printed after ""Do"" returned and the log closed.
  Nothing was left to write it.
end note

note bottom of ATAIL
  **It printed where the log could not see it.** The
  block went straight to ""os.Stderr"", so it reached
  the operator who already had it and never the file
  the same paragraph sends them to. Routed through
  ui, it is teed and stays greppable.
end note

note bottom of FIELDS
  Fields are written sorted so two runs of the same command produce
  transcripts that diff to only what actually differed — the property that
  makes a transcript useful for comparing a working run against a broken
  one.
end note


@enduml
